Vulnerability/Misconfiguration Link: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-114613 (CVE-2026-114613 / GHSA — msgpack: Denial of Service via memory leak during map deserialization)
Severity: HIGH (CVSS per Trivy/AVD; DoS via memory exhaustion, no RCE/data exposure)
Summary: Trivy flags msgpack 1.1.2 as vulnerable in the built image. This is not the application's msgpack dependency (which is pinned to >=1.2.3 via our own pip install and confirmed patched). The flagged copy is pip's internally vendored fallback, located at pip/_vendor/msgpack/, used solely by pip's own HTTP response cache deserialization during package installs. As of pip 26.2.2 (current latest), pip/_vendor/vendor.txt still pins msgpack==1.1.2; no upstream pip release has bumped this yet. This is a known, currently-unresolved upstream issue — confirmed independently by multiple other open-source projects (e.g. netboxlabs/orb-agent, felddy/weewx-docker, OpenHands/software-agent-sdk) hitting the identical finding this week with the same root cause.
image

Ignore Reason:
The vulnerable code is not reachable by application code. pip._vendor.msgpack is never imported or invoked by our application — it is only exercised internally by pip during its own package-install/cache operations, which do not run in the deployed container (pip is a build-time tool, not a runtime dependency of the service). Additionally, the memory-leak vulnerability lives in msgpack's C extension; pip vendors only the pure-Python fallback implementation, meaning the vulnerable code path is likely not even present in the vendored copy. No upstream fix exists yet (no pip release vendors msgpack >=1.2.3), so there is no actionable remediation available at this time.
Risk Assessment: Low. Exploitation requires the vulnerable code to be reachable and fed attacker-controlled msgpack-encoded input. Since pip._vendor.msgpack is never invoked by runtime application logic and pip itself is not exposed to untrusted network input in this image's runtime context, there is no practical attack surface. Application-facing msgpack usage is on the patched >=1.2.3 release. This is an accepted, time-boxed residual risk consistent with how other projects in the ecosystem have classified the same finding (pip-vendored, non-reachable, upstream-blocked).
